        "notes": "TABLE RECOMPUTED ROW BY ROW AND IT IS CORRECT: (4)->(3), dim 1, trivial 1; (3,1)->(3)+(2,1), dim 3, trivial 1; (2,2)->(2,1), dim 2, trivial 0; (2,1,1)->(2,1)+(1^3), dim 3, trivial 0; (1^4)->(1^3), dim 1, trivial 0. Independent invariant: sum of dim^2 = 1+9+4+9+1 = 24 = 4!, passes. Conjugate pairing {(4),(1^4)}, {(3,1),(2,1,1)} and self-conjugate (2,2) also correct. TWO FINDINGS OUTSIDE THE TABLE. (1) UNEXPLAINED FACTOR OF 2: the proof derives dim_half-sym = 1+3+1 = 5 and the trivial factor 2/5, then writes 'effective cell count = (5/2) x (2/5) = 1' calling 5/2 the half-sym count. Its own half-sym count is 5, and 5 x (2/5) = 2, not 1. The 5/2 appears nowhere above it, and the sibling witness reports h0_equals_5_over_2 = FALSE. (2) THREE HYPERBOLIC-GEOMETRY ERRORS IN ONE PARENTHETICAL, recomputed at 20 dps: G = Cl_2(pi/2) = 0.9159655942, while Lambda(pi/4) = 0.4579827971 = G/2, so G = Lambda(pi/4) is wrong by a factor 2 (correct: G = 2 Lambda(pi/4)); V_oct = 8 Lambda(pi/4) = 4G = 3.6638624, so G = (1/2) V_oct is wrong (correct: G = V_oct/4); and Vol(B_3) = 8G = 7.3277248 = 2 V_oct, i.e. the Borromean complement is TWO regular ideal octahedra, not eight \u2014 the 8 in 8G counts Catalan-constant units. Consequently the 8-fold tessellation, the per-cell ratio 1/8, and one of the EIGHT ideal octahedral cells are all wrong; with 2 cells the ratio is 1/2. This is the documented Lambda-vs-Cl_2 hazard (right value, wrong function name) with a cell count inferred from the wrong name. PROPOSED: Vol(B_3) = 8G = 2 V_oct with G = Cl_2(pi/2) = 2 Lambda(pi/4) = V_oct/4, and rebuild the cell argument on 2 cells."
